# SRG-APP-000383-WSR-000175  -  CAT II
# The web server must prohibit or restrict the use of nonsecure or
# unnecessary ports, protocols, modules, and/or services.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000383-WSR-000175
group:           V-206428
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-001762
control_rev5:    CM-7

contribution:    Splunk decides which ports it opens and what protocol each one speaks: it
                 can be configured with no connectionless input at all, and it can require
                 TLS on the management port, the web port, and the receiving port with a
                 declared protocol version list and cipher suite.

governs:         inputs.conf - Configures inputs, distributed inputs such as forwarders, and file system
                 monitoring.
governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           inputs.conf [udp:<port>] * absent
check:           server.conf [sslConfig] enableSplunkdSSL in true|1
check:           web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL in true|1
check:           server.conf [sslConfig] sslVersions matches ^[[:space:]]*(tls1\.2|tls1\.3)([[:space:]]*,[[:space:]]*(tls1\.2|tls1\.3))*[[:space:]]*$
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          inputs.conf [udp:<port>] disabled https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.2-configuration-file-reference/inputs.conf
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] cipherSuite https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] enableSplunkdSSL https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/secure-splunk-platform-communications-with-transport-layer-security-certificates/configure-tls-certificates-for-inter-splunk-communication
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] sslVersions https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/secure-splunk-platform-communications-with-transport-layer-security-certificates/configure-tls-protocol-version-support-for-secure-connections-between-splunk-platform-instances
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Which ports are reachable from where, carried by the host firewall and the
                 network; the application can close a listener but cannot filter who
                 reaches an open one.
external:        The protocol versions and ciphers the underlying TLS library actually
                 offers, carried by the platform build, since the shipped cipher default
                 comes from the OpenSSL binary.
external:        Approval of the ports and protocols in use for a production system, which
                 is an organizational determination recorded against the ports, protocols,
                 and services registration.

citation:        SPLK-CL-000270 and SPLK-CL-000290 check and fix text;
                 inputs.conf.spec:1314 ([udp:<port>]), server.conf.spec:724
                 (enableSplunkdSSL), web.conf.spec:70 (enableSplunkWebSSL, default false),
                 server.conf.spec:765 and 775 (sslVersions, default varies by shipped
                 file), server.conf.spec:940 (cipherSuite, otherwise the OpenSSL default)
finding:         A UDP input remains configured, or a management, web, or receiving
                 listener does not require TLS with a declared version list and cipher
                 suite, so the application still offers a nonsecure or unnecessary port or
                 protocol.
